exec(open('/home/claude/work/gita/gc.py').read())
G3=lambda lab,cap,hi=None: S(lab,'K',None,cap,B,GUNA3(160,90,.9)+(GLOW({'s':120,'r':160,'t':200}[hi],90,24,'#ffd60a',.7) if hi else ''))
V=[
X(1,'THE SUPREME KNOWLEDGE',
 S('Krishna about to reveal the highest knowledge','K','book','PARAM JÑĀNAM'),
 'The Lord said: I will tell you again the supreme knowledge, the best of all knowledge, knowing which all the sages have gone from here to the highest perfection.',
 [('param bhūyaḥ pravakṣyāmi','<b>the supreme, again I will tell</b>'),('jñānānām jñānam uttamam','<b>the best knowledge of all knowledge</b>'),('yat jñātvā munayaḥ sarve','<b>knowing which all the sages</b>'),('parām siddhim itaḥ gatāḥ','<b>went from here to supreme perfection</b>')],
 'Chapter 14 opens with Kṛṣṇa promising more of the highest knowledge.',
 'This chapter explains the three guṇas, the qualities of nature introduced earlier (3.27, 7.12–14, 13.21). Commentators note “again”: the teaching spirals, returning to key ideas at deeper levels. Understanding the guṇas is practical psychology as well as philosophy.',
 'Understanding how our moods and habits work is itself a kind of liberation.',
 'The <b>best of all knowledge</b>.'),
X(2,'BEYOND CREATION AND DISSOLUTION',
 S('Sages untouched while worlds are created and dissolved','Y','wheel','NOT DISTURBED'),
 '“Those who take refuge in this knowledge and attain my nature are not born at the time of creation, nor are they disturbed at the time of dissolution.”',
 [('idam jñānam upāśritya','<b>taking refuge in this knowledge</b>'),('mama sādharmyam āgatāḥ','<b>having reached likeness with me</b>'),('sarge api na upajāyante','<b>are not born even at creation</b>'),('pralaye na vyathanti ca','<b>nor disturbed at dissolution</b>')],
 'This knowledge frees from the cosmic cycle.',
 '<i>Sādharmya</i>, “sharing my nature”: Rāmānuja stresses this as becoming like God in purity, while remaining distinct; Śaṅkara as identity with Brahman. Either way, the cosmic cycles no longer touch the freed soul.',
 'True knowledge brings a peace that even the end of the world cannot shake.',
 'Beyond <b>creation and dissolution</b>.'),
X(3,'MY WOMB AND MY SEED',
 S('Krishna placing a seed in the great womb of nature','K','seed','MAHAD-BRAHMA'),
 '“Great Brahman (mahat-prakṛti) is my womb; in it I place the seed. From that comes the birth of all beings, Bhārata.”',
 [('mama yoniḥ mahat brahma','<b>great Brahman is my womb</b>'),('tasmin garbham dadhāmi aham','<b>in it I place the seed</b>'),('sambhavaḥ sarva-bhūtānām','<b>the birth of all beings</b>'),('tataḥ bhavati bhārata','<b>comes from that</b>')],
 'Creation as birth: nature the womb, God the seed.',
 'Here “great Brahman” means prakṛti, primal nature, not the Absolute. Commentators: matter alone cannot produce life; the Lord’s seed of consciousness is needed.',
 'Every living being carries a divine seed. Treat life with reverence.',
 'Nature the womb, <b>God the seed</b>.'),
X(4,'THE FATHER WHO GIVES THE SEED',
 S('Krishna as the seed-giving father of all forms','K','family','BĪJA-PRADAḤ PITĀ'),
 '“Whatever forms are born in all wombs, son of Kuntī, great Brahman is their womb, and I am the seed-giving father.”',
 [('sarva-yoniṣu kaunteya','<b>in all wombs</b>'),('mūrtayaḥ sambhavanti yāḥ','<b>whatever forms are born</b>'),('tāsām brahma mahat yoniḥ','<b>great Brahman is their womb</b>'),('aham bīja-pradaḥ pitā','<b>I am the seed-giving father</b>')],
 'God as father of all beings.',
 'Commentators note every species, not only humans. All living things share one father. A basis for kindness to all creatures.',
 'All creatures are family. That changes how we treat them.',
 'I am <b>the father of all</b>.'),
X(5,'THREE QUALITIES BIND',
 G3('Three coloured cords of sattva, rajas and tamas binding a soul','THEY BIND'),
 '“Sattva, rajas and tamas: these guṇas born of prakṛti bind the imperishable embodied one in the body, mighty-armed one.”',
 [('sattvam rajaḥ tamaḥ iti','<b>sattva, rajas, tamas</b>'),('guṇāḥ prakṛti-sambhavāḥ','<b>the guṇas born of nature</b>'),('nibadhnanti mahā-bāho','<b>bind, mighty-armed</b>'),('dehe dehinam avyayam','<b>the imperishable embodied one in the body</b>')],
 'The three guṇas introduced: they bind the soul.',
 '<i>Guṇa</i> means quality, and also rope or strand. All three bind, even sattva. Commentators: sattva is light, rajas is activity, tamas is inertia. Everything in nature is a mix of the three.',
 'Our moods are woven from these three strands. Learning to see them is the first step to freedom.',
 'Three strands <b>that bind</b>.'),
X(6,'SATTVA: THE GOLDEN CHAIN',
 G3('Sattva glowing pure and bright, still a chain','SATTVA','s'),
 '“Of these, sattva, being pure, is illuminating and free from ill. It binds by attachment to happiness and to knowledge, sinless one.”',
 [('tatra sattvam nirmalatvāt','<b>there, sattva, being pure</b>'),('prakāśakam anāmayam','<b>illuminating, free from ill</b>'),('sukha-saṅgena badhnāti','<b>binds by attachment to happiness</b>'),('jñāna-saṅgena ca anagha','<b>and by attachment to knowledge</b>')],
 'Even goodness binds, through attachment to happiness and knowledge.',
 'Commentators call sattva a golden chain: still a chain. Pride in being good or learned is subtle bondage. Yet sattva is the best starting point for freedom.',
 'Notice attachment even to good things: being right, being calm, knowing more.',
 'Even goodness <b>can bind</b>.'),
X(7,'RAJAS: THE DRIVE TO ACT',
 G3('Rajas as a red restless fire driving endless action','RAJAS','r'),
 '“Know rajas as passion, arising from craving and attachment. It binds the embodied one, son of Kuntī, by attachment to action.”',
 [('rajaḥ rāga-ātmakam viddhi','<b>know rajas as passion</b>'),('tṛṣṇā-saṅga-samudbhavam','<b>born of craving and attachment</b>'),('tat nibadhnāti kaunteya','<b>it binds</b>'),('karma-saṅgena dehinam','<b>the embodied, by attachment to action</b>')],
 'Rajas binds through restless activity.',
 'Rajas is the energy of ambition and busyness. Commentators: it is not bad in itself, but its craving keeps the mind restless. Modern life is often called rājasika.',
 'Busyness can be a trap. Notice when you act from restlessness rather than purpose.',
 'Rajas binds <b>by restless action</b>.'),
X(8,'TAMAS: THE FOG',
 G3('Tamas as a dark fog of sleep and laziness','TAMAS','t'),
 '“Know tamas as born of ignorance, deluding all embodied beings. It binds, Bhārata, through negligence, laziness and sleep.”',
 [('tamaḥ tu ajñāna-jam viddhi','<b>know tamas as born of ignorance</b>'),('mohanam sarva-dehinām','<b>deluding all embodied beings</b>'),('pramāda-ālasya-nidrābhiḥ','<b>by negligence, laziness, sleep</b>'),('tat nibadhnāti bhārata','<b>it binds</b>')],
 'Tamas binds through dullness.',
 'Three marks: <i>pramāda</i> (carelessness), <i>ālasya</i> (sloth), <i>nidrā</i> (excess sleep). Commentators: tamas is the heaviest; rajas at least moves, and can be redirected.',
 'When you feel heavy and dull, gentle action is the cure.',
 'Tamas binds <b>by dullness</b>.'),
X(9,'WHAT EACH ATTACHES TO',
 G3('Sattva to happiness, rajas to action, tamas to negligence','3 ATTACHMENTS'),
 '“Sattva attaches one to happiness, rajas to action, Bhārata; but tamas, veiling knowledge, attaches one to negligence.”',
 [('sattvam sukhe sañjayati','<b>sattva attaches to happiness</b>'),('rajaḥ karmaṇi bhārata','<b>rajas to action</b>'),('jñānam āvṛtya tu tamaḥ','<b>but tamas, veiling knowledge</b>'),('pramāde sañjayati uta','<b>attaches to negligence</b>')],
 'A summary of the three bindings.',
 'Commentators offer a ladder: rise from tamas to rajas (get moving), from rajas to sattva (calm and clarity), and then beyond sattva too.',
 'Know which guṇa holds you today. Then you know what to do next.',
 'Happiness, action, <b>negligence</b>.'),
X(10,'THEY COMPETE',
 G3('Three guna balls jostling, one rising above the others','WHO WINS?'),
 '“Sattva prevails, overpowering rajas and tamas, Bhārata; rajas, overpowering sattva and tamas; and likewise tamas, overpowering sattva and rajas.”',
 [('rajaḥ tamaḥ ca abhibhūya','<b>overpowering rajas and tamas</b>'),('sattvam bhavati bhārata','<b>sattva arises</b>'),('rajaḥ sattvam tamaḥ ca eva','<b>rajas, over sattva and tamas</b>'),('tamaḥ sattvam rajaḥ tathā','<b>tamas, over sattva and rajas</b>')],
 'The three are always mixed, one dominating at a time.',
 'Commentators: the mix changes through the day. Morning may be sāttvika, afternoon rājasika, night tāmasika. We can tilt the balance by food, company and habit.',
 'You can influence which guṇa rules. Small choices tip the balance.',
 'The three <b>take turns ruling</b>.'),
X(11,'SIGNS OF SATTVA',
 G3('Light shining from all the gates of the body','LIGHT!','s'),
 '“When the light of knowledge shines through all the gates of this body, then know that sattva has increased.”',
 [('sarva-dvāreṣu dehe asmin','<b>in all the gates of this body</b>'),('prakāśaḥ upajāyate','<b>light arises</b>'),('jñānam yadā tadā vidyāt','<b>when knowledge, then know</b>'),('vivṛddham sattvam iti uta','<b>that sattva has increased</b>')],
 'How to recognise sattva: clarity in every sense.',
 'The “gates” are the senses (5.13). Commentators: when sattva rules, we see, hear and think clearly. Peace and alertness together.',
 'Notice moments of clarity. That is sattva shining.',
 'Clarity in every sense: <b>sattva</b>.'),
X(12,'SIGNS OF RAJAS',
 G3('A busy, greedy, restless figure','RESTLESS!','r'),
 '“Greed, activity, undertaking of actions, restlessness and longing arise when rajas increases, best of the Bharatas.”',
 [('lobhaḥ pravṛttiḥ ārambhaḥ','<b>greed, activity, undertaking</b>'),('karmaṇām aśamaḥ spṛhā','<b>of actions, restlessness, longing</b>'),('rajasi etāni jāyante','<b>these arise in rajas</b>'),('vivṛddhe bharata-ṛṣabha','<b>when it increases</b>')],
 'How to recognise rajas: greed and restlessness.',
 'Commentators list the marks: always starting new projects, never at rest, wanting more. Useful energy, but tiring.',
 'When you can’t stop starting things, rajas is high. Pause.',
 'Greed and restlessness: <b>rajas</b>.'),
X(13,'SIGNS OF TAMAS',
 G3('A dark, sleepy, confused figure','FOG','t'),
 '“Darkness, inertia, negligence and delusion arise when tamas increases, joy of the Kurus.”',
 [('aprakāśaḥ apravṛttiḥ ca','<b>darkness and inactivity</b>'),('pramādaḥ mohaḥ eva ca','<b>negligence and delusion</b>'),('tamasi etāni jāyante','<b>these arise in tamas</b>'),('vivṛddhe kuru-nandana','<b>when it increases</b>')],
 'How to recognise tamas: fog and inertia.',
 'Commentators: tamas makes everything seem pointless and heavy. It is the hardest to see from inside.',
 'If everything feels heavy and dull, move a little. Walk, clean, start small.',
 'Fog and inertia: <b>tamas</b>.'),
X(14,'DYING IN SATTVA',
 G3('A person departing in a bright state, rising to pure worlds','PURE WORLDS','s'),
 '“If the embodied one meets death when sattva prevails, he goes to the pure worlds of those who know the highest.”',
 [('yadā sattve pravṛddhe tu','<b>when sattva prevails</b>'),('pralayam yāti deha-bhṛt','<b>the embodied one meets death</b>'),('tadā uttama-vidām lokān','<b>then to the worlds of the knowers of the highest</b>'),('amalān pratipadyate','<b>pure, he reaches</b>')],
 'The state at death shapes the next life.',
 'This connects to 8.6: what dominates at death shapes rebirth. Commentators: cultivate sattva in life to depart in it.',
 'Live in clarity and kindness, and your end will be bright.',
 'Departing in sattva: <b>pure worlds</b>.'),
X(15,'DYING IN RAJAS OR TAMAS',
 G3('Departing in restlessness or in darkness, leading to different births','BACK AGAIN'),
 '“Meeting death in rajas, one is born among those attached to action; dying in tamas, one is born in the wombs of the deluded.”',
 [('rajasi pralayam gatvā','<b>meeting death in rajas</b>'),('karma-saṅgiṣu jāyate','<b>is born among those attached to action</b>'),('tathā pralīnaḥ tamasi','<b>so, dying in tamas</b>'),('mūḍha-yoniṣu jāyate','<b>is born in deluded wombs</b>')],
 'Restless or dull states lead to corresponding births.',
 'Commentators read “deluded wombs” as lower or confused states of life. The point is moral cause and effect, not fear.',
 'Today’s habits shape tomorrow’s life. Choose well.',
 'As you depart, <b>so you return</b>.'),
X(16,'THE FRUITS OF EACH',
 G3('Three trees with different fruits: pure, painful, dark','FRUITS'),
 '“The fruit of good action is said to be sāttvika and pure; the fruit of rajas is pain; the fruit of tamas is ignorance.”',
 [('karmaṇaḥ sukṛtasya āhuḥ','<b>of good action, they say</b>'),('sāttvikam nirmalam phalam','<b>the fruit is sāttvika, pure</b>'),('rajasaḥ tu phalam duḥkham','<b>but of rajas, pain</b>'),('ajñānam tamasaḥ phalam','<b>ignorance, of tamas</b>')],
 'Each guṇa bears its own fruit.',
 'Rajas looks productive but ends in pain (exhaustion, loss, craving). Commentators: judge actions by their fruits over time.',
 'Look at where your efforts lead: to peace, pain, or fog?',
 'Purity, pain, <b>or ignorance</b>.'),
X(17,'WHAT EACH PRODUCES',
 G3('Knowledge from sattva, greed from rajas, delusion from tamas','FROM EACH'),
 '“From sattva arises knowledge; from rajas, greed; from tamas arise negligence, delusion and ignorance.”',
 [('sattvāt sañjāyate jñānam','<b>from sattva, knowledge</b>'),('rajasaḥ lobhaḥ eva ca','<b>from rajas, greed</b>'),('pramāda-mohau tamasaḥ','<b>negligence and delusion from tamas</b>'),('bhavataḥ ajñānam eva ca','<b>and ignorance</b>')],
 'Another summary: what each guṇa produces in the mind.',
 'Commentators: by watching what arises in us, we can tell which guṇa is ruling.',
 'Knowledge, greed or confusion: which is growing in you?',
 'Knowledge, greed, <b>delusion</b>.'),
X(18,'UP, MIDDLE, DOWN',
 G3('Three arrows: sattva up, rajas level, tamas down','↑ → ↓'),
 '“Those established in sattva go upward; the rājasika stay in the middle; the tāmasika, abiding in the lowest guṇa, go downward.”',
 [('ūrdhvam gacchanti sattva-sthāḥ','<b>those in sattva go upward</b>'),('madhye tiṣṭhanti rājasāḥ','<b>the rājasika stay in the middle</b>'),('jaghanya-guṇa-vṛtti-sthāḥ','<b>those in the lowest guṇa</b>'),('adhaḥ gacchanti tāmasāḥ','<b>the tāmasika go down</b>')],
 'The direction each guṇa takes us.',
 'A simple map: up, level, down. Commentators read “up” as higher worlds or higher consciousness; “middle” as the human world of striving.',
 'Ask: is this choice taking me up, keeping me level, or pulling me down?',
 'Up, middle, <b>down</b>.'),
X(19,'SEEING THE GUṆAS AS DOERS',
 S('A seer watching the three guṇas act, himself beyond them','Y','guna','BEYOND'),
 '“When the seer sees no doer other than the guṇas, and knows what is beyond the guṇas, he attains my being.”',
 [('na anyam guṇebhyaḥ kartāram','<b>no doer other than the guṇas</b>'),('yadā draṣṭā anupaśyati','<b>when the seer sees</b>'),('guṇebhyaḥ ca param vetti','<b>and knows what is beyond the guṇas</b>'),('mat-bhāvam saḥ adhigacchati','<b>he attains my being</b>')],
 'Freedom comes from seeing the guṇas as the doers, and knowing what lies beyond.',
 'This repeats 3.27–28 and 13.29. Commentators: the seer watches like a spectator at a play, knowing he is not the actors.',
 'Watch your moods as weather passing over you. You are the sky.',
 'Guṇas act; <b>the seer is free</b>.'),
X(20,'BEYOND THE THREE',
 S('A person stepping beyond the three guna circles into light','Y','light','GUṆĀTĪTA'),
 '“Going beyond these three guṇas, from which the body arises, the embodied one, freed from birth, death, old age and sorrow, attains immortality.”',
 [('guṇān etān atītya trīn','<b>going beyond these three guṇas</b>'),('dehī deha-samudbhavān','<b>the embodied, from which bodies arise</b>'),('janma-mṛtyu-jarā-duḥkhaiḥ','<b>from birth, death, age, sorrow</b>'),('vimuktaḥ amṛtam aśnute','<b>freed, attains immortality</b>')],
 'The goal: beyond all three guṇas.',
 '<i>Guṇātīta</i>, beyond the guṇas, names the ideal of this chapter. Commentators: not destroying the guṇas but being free of their grip.',
 'Freedom is not changing your nature but no longer being ruled by it.',
 'Beyond the three: <b>immortality</b>.'),
X(21,'WHAT ARE THE MARKS?',
 S('Arjuna asking how to recognise one beyond the gunas','A','guna','HOW TO KNOW?'),
 'Arjuna said: By what marks is one known who has gone beyond these three guṇas, Lord? How does he behave, and how does he go beyond the three guṇas?',
 [('kaiḥ liṅgaiḥ trīn guṇān etān','<b>by what marks, these three guṇas</b>'),('atītaḥ bhavati prabho','<b>is one beyond, Lord</b>'),('kim ācāraḥ','<b>what is his conduct?</b>'),('katham ca etān trīn guṇān ativartate','<b>and how does he transcend them?</b>')],
 'Arjuna asks three practical questions.',
 'This mirrors 2.54, where Arjuna asked about the sthitaprajña. Commentators: Arjuna always turns teaching into practice.',
 'Ask how ideas look in real life. That is the test of understanding.',
 'What does it <b>look like</b>?'),
X(22,'NEITHER HATING NOR LONGING',
 S('A calm person as light, activity and delusion come and go','Y','guna','NA DVEṢṬI'),
 'The Lord said: One who does not hate clarity, activity or delusion when they arise, Pāṇḍava, nor long for them when they cease,',
 [('prakāśam ca pravṛttim ca','<b>clarity and activity</b>'),('moham eva ca pāṇḍava','<b>and delusion</b>'),('na dveṣṭi sampravṛttāni','<b>does not hate when present</b>'),('na nivṛttāni kāṅkṣati','<b>nor longs for them when gone</b>')],
 'The first mark: accepting all three states without hate or longing.',
 'Clarity (sattva), activity (rajas), delusion (tamas): the free person neither clings to the good state nor hates the bad. Commentators: this is equanimity toward one’s own mind.',
 'Don’t fight your moods or chase them. Let them come and go.',
 'Neither hating <b>nor longing</b>.'),
X(23,'SITTING AS A WITNESS',
 S('A person sitting calmly, watching the gunas move','Y','light','UDĀSĪNAVAT'),
 '“…who sits as if indifferent, unshaken by the guṇas, knowing ‘the guṇas alone are acting’, who stands firm and does not waver,”',
 [('udāsīna-vat āsīnaḥ','<b>sitting as if indifferent</b>'),('guṇaiḥ yaḥ na vicālyate','<b>who is not shaken by the guṇas</b>'),('guṇāḥ vartante iti eva','<b>knowing “the guṇas act”</b>'),('yaḥ avatiṣṭhati na iṅgate','<b>who stands firm, does not waver</b>')],
 'The second mark: a steady witness.',
 '<i>Udāsīna</i>, literally “sitting above”, like the Lord in 9.9. Commentators: not cold detachment but serene overview.',
 'Practise watching rather than reacting. A breath of distance helps.',
 'Sitting above, <b>unshaken</b>.'),
X(24,'THE SAME IN ALL',
 S('A person treating gold and clay, praise and blame alike','Y','scale','SAMA'),
 '“…the same in pain and pleasure, abiding in the Self, to whom clay, stone and gold are alike, the same toward the dear and the not-dear, steady, the same in blame and praise,”',
 [('sama-duḥkha-sukhaḥ sva-sthaḥ','<b>same in pain and pleasure, self-abiding</b>'),('sama-loṣṭa-aśma-kāñcanaḥ','<b>clay, stone, gold alike</b>'),('tulya-priya-apriyaḥ dhīraḥ','<b>equal to dear and not-dear, steady</b>'),('tulya-nindā-ātma-saṃstutiḥ','<b>same in blame and praise</b>')],
 'The third mark: evenness everywhere.',
 '<i>Svastha</i>, “established in oneself”, is also the everyday word for “healthy”. Commentators enjoy the link: true health is resting in the Self.',
 'To be healthy is to be at home in yourself.',
 'The same in <b>praise and blame</b>.'),
X(25,'FRIEND AND FOE ALIKE',
 S('A person calm before friends and enemies, honour and insult','Y','family','GUṆĀTĪTAḤ'),
 '“…the same in honour and dishonour, the same to friend and foe, who has given up all selfish undertakings: he is said to have gone beyond the guṇas.”',
 [('māna-apamānayoḥ tulyaḥ','<b>the same in honour and dishonour</b>'),('tulyaḥ mitra-ari-pakṣayoḥ','<b>the same to friend and foe</b>'),('sarva-ārambha-parityāgī','<b>giving up all undertakings</b>'),('guṇa-atītaḥ saḥ ucyate','<b>he is called beyond the guṇas</b>')],
 'The portrait ends.',
 'Compare 12.18–19 (the devotee) and 2.55–72 (the sthitaprajña): the same ideal from different angles. Commentators: the sage, the devotee and the guṇātīta are one person.',
 'Different paths describe the same freedom. That is encouraging.',
 'Beyond <b>the guṇas</b>.'),
X(26,'BY UNWAVERING DEVOTION',
 S('A devotee serving Krishna, rising beyond the three gunas','KA','heart','BHAKTI-YOGA'),
 '“One who serves me with the yoga of unwavering devotion, going beyond these guṇas, becomes fit for oneness with Brahman.”',
 [('mām ca yaḥ avyabhicāreṇa','<b>and who, unwaveringly, me</b>'),('bhakti-yogena sevate','<b>serves with the yoga of devotion</b>'),('saḥ guṇān samatītya etān','<b>he, going beyond these guṇas</b>'),('brahma-bhūyāya kalpate','<b>becomes fit to be Brahman</b>')],
 'Arjuna’s “how?” is answered: by devotion.',
 'A key verse: devotion is the means to go beyond the guṇas. Commentators of all schools note how the Gītā joins bhakti and the highest knowledge.',
 'Love is the simplest way out of the tangle of moods.',
 'Devotion <b>carries you beyond</b>.'),
X(27,'THE ABODE OF BRAHMAN',
 S('Krishna as the ground of Brahman, eternal dharma and bliss','K','om','PRATIṢṬHĀ'),
 '“For I am the abode of Brahman, the immortal and imperishable, of eternal dharma, and of absolute bliss.”',
 [('brahmaṇaḥ hi pratiṣṭhā aham','<b>I am the abode of Brahman</b>'),('amṛtasya avyayasya ca','<b>of the immortal and imperishable</b>'),('śāśvatasya ca dharmasya','<b>and of eternal dharma</b>'),('sukhasya aikāntikasya ca','<b>and of absolute bliss</b>')],
 'The chapter ends with Kṛṣṇa as the ground of all.',
 'A much-debated verse: “I am the abode of Brahman.” Śaṅkara reads it as identity: Kṛṣṇa is Brahman itself. Rāmānuja reads: the Lord is the support even of the freed soul’s Brahman-state. Gauḍīya Vaiṣṇavas read the personal Lord as the source of the impersonal Brahman.',
 'Behind every ideal, truth, dharma, bliss, is a single source.',
 'I am the abode <b>of Brahman and bliss</b>.'),
]
